1. Project overview
   Your application is a Node.js command-line tool that can:

- Check password strength.
- Check whether a password appears in known breach data using the Have I Been Pwned (HIBP) API.
- Generate strong random passwords.
- Send generated passwords to Slack.
- Run automated tests and use Docker for packaging.
  The main idea is to keep each responsibility in a separate file, making the application easier to maintain and test.

2. Explanation of each file
1. src/validator.js — Password strength checker
   Purpose: Checks whether a user-provided password follows the password policy.
   What it does:

- Checks the minimum length of 12 characters.
- Checks for uppercase letters.
- Checks for lowercase letters.
- Checks for numbers.
- Checks for special characters.
- Returns whether the password is valid and a list of errors if it isn't.
  Main function: validatePassword(password)
  EX: validatePassword("hello123");

The function returns an object containing valid: false and the reasons the password failed.
Note for your notebook: This file handles local password validation. It doesn't make API calls or generate passwords.

2. src/hibp.js — Password breach checker
   Purpose: Checks whether a password has appeared in known password breach data.
   What it does:
1. Takes the password as input.
1. Calculates its SHA-1 hash locally.
1. Splits the hash into a five-character prefix and the remaining suffix.
1. Sends only the prefix to the HIBP API.
1. Receives matching hash suffixes.
1. Compares the full hash locally and returns whether a match was found and how many times it appeared.
   Main function: checkPasswordBreach(password)
   Important concept — k-anonymity: The actual password and its complete hash are not sent to HIBP. Only the first five characters of the hash are sent, helping protect the password's privacy.
   Note for your notebook: This file handles external API communication and breach detection, not password strength validation.
1. src/generator.js — Secure password generator
   Purpose: Generates random passwords using Node.js's cryptographically secure random functions.
   What it does:

- Generates passwords with a default length of 20 characters.
- Allows a length between 16 and 24 characters.
- Ensures at least one uppercase letter, lowercase letter, and digit.
- Includes at least one special character by default.
- Supports the noSymbols option.
- Shuffles the characters so required character types aren't always in fixed positions.
  Main function: generatePassword(options)

Eg: generatePassword({ length: 18, noSymbols: true });

Important concept: It uses Node.js crypto.randomInt() rather than Math.random() to select characters securely.
Note for your notebook: This file generates passwords. It doesn't check the password against HIBP or send it to Slack.

4. src/safe-generator.js — Generate and verify passwords
   Purpose: Combines password generation with the HIBP breach checker.
   What it does:
1. Generates a password using generator.js.
1. Checks the password using hibp.js.
1. If the password appears in breach data, it discards that password and tries again.
1. Stops after a maximum of five attempts by default.
1. Returns a password only when HIBP reports no match.
1. Throws an error if the API fails or all attempts find breached passwords.
   Main function: generateUnbreachedPassword(options)
   It also accepts injected generator and breach-check functions, which makes it easier to write unit tests without making real API requests.
   Note for your notebook: This file coordinates password generation and breach checking. It adds a safeguard so a password isn't released when its breach status is unknown.

1. src/slack.js — Slack integration
   Purpose: Sends a generated password to a Slack channel through an Incoming Webhook.
   What it does:

- Reads SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL from environment variables.
- Checks that the URL uses HTTPS and the expected Slack webhook hostname.
- Sends a POST request containing the password in the message body.
- Throws an error if the webhook is missing, invalid, or returns a failed response.
- Returns a success result if Slack accepts the message.
  Main function: sendPasswordToSlack(password)
  Example command: node src/cli.js generate --length 20 --send-slack

Note for your notebook: This file handles password delivery to Slack. It doesn't generate or validate passwords itself.
Remember that Slack messages can be retained or visible to workspace administrators, so only send passwords to an authorized destination.

6. src/cli.js — Main application entry point
   Purpose: Connects all the modules and allows users to operate the application through terminal commands.
   This is the main file a user runs.
   What it does:

- Loads environment variables using dotenv.
- Parses command-line arguments.
- Supports the check and generate commands.
- Reads passwords without displaying the characters while typing.
- Calls the validator and HIBP checker for password checks.
- Calls safe-generator.js to generate and verify passwords.
- Calls slack.js when --send-slack is specified.
- Handles errors without intentionally printing passwords or webhook URLs.
  Important functions
  Function Responsibility
  parseArgs(args): Reads options such as --length, --no-symbols, and --send-slack.
  promptHidden(question): Accepts password input without echoing it to the terminal.
  run(): Coordinates the selected command and calls the appropriate modules.
  Example commands
  node src/cli.js check
  node src/cli.js generate
  node src/cli.js generate --length 18
  node src/cli.js generate --length 18 --no-symbols
  node src/cli.js generate --length 20 --send-slack

Note for your notebook: This file is the controller of the application. It connects all the modules and determines what happens based on the user's command.
One detail to remember: we added safe-generator.js recently, and its live CLI generation behavior was not displaying output during our last debugging session. The automated tests passed, but that particular behavior still needs verification. 3. Test files
The test/ folder contains automated tests. These tests check the application's behavior without requiring you to manually try every scenario. 7. test/validator.test.js
Purpose: Tests password strength validation.
It checks:

- Valid passwords are accepted.
- Short passwords are rejected.
- Missing uppercase, lowercase, numbers, and symbols are detected.
- Multiple errors can be returned together.
- Non-string input is rejected.
  Note: Tests the validator.js module.

8. test/hibp.test.js
   Purpose: Tests the HIBP breach-checking functionality.
   It checks:

- A password matching the mocked breach response is detected.
- A password absent from the response is reported as not found.
- API failures are handled.
- Non-string input is rejected.
  Important: These tests mock the API response; they don't prove that a live HIBP request always works.
  Note: Tests the hibp.js module.

9. test/generator.test.js
   Purpose: Tests password generation.
   It checks:

- The default password length.
- Custom lengths within the allowed range.
- Required character types.
- Generation without symbols.
- Invalid lengths and non-integer lengths.
  Note: Tests the generator.js module.

10. test/slack.test.js
    Purpose: Tests Slack message delivery without sending real messages.
    It checks:

- Successful webhook delivery.
- Missing webhook configuration.
- Invalid webhook URLs.
- Slack delivery failures.
  The tests mock fetch, allowing them to run without contacting Slack.
  Note: Tests the slack.js module.

11. test/safe-generator.test.js
    Purpose: Tests the process of generating a password and checking it against HIBP before releasing it.
    It checks:

- An unbreached password is returned.
- A breached password triggers a retry.
- The retry limit is respected.
- HIBP failures stop the process.
- Invalid API responses are rejected.
- Invalid retry limits are rejected.
  Note: Tests the safe-generator.js module and its retry behavior.

4. Configuration and project files
5. .env — Environment variables
   Purpose: Stores configuration values and secrets outside the source code.
   Example: SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=https://hooks.slack.com/services/...

The application reads this value using dotenv.
Note: Keeps credentials separate from the code. Never commit the real .env file to Git. 13. .gitignore — Files Git should ignore
Purpose: Prevents sensitive or unnecessary files from being included in Git commits.
Your file contains:

node_modules/
.env
coverage/

- node_modules/ — installed dependencies.
- .env — secrets and local configuration.
- coverage/ — generated test coverage reports.
  Note: Protects secrets from accidental commits and keeps the repository cleaner.

14. package.json — Project configuration
    Purpose: Defines your Node.js project's metadata, dependencies, and scripts.
    It contains information such as:

- Project name and version.
- Installed packages, such as dotenv.
- The npm test command.
- Other project scripts and configuration.
  For example:
  "scripts": {
  "test": "node --test"
  }

This lets you run all the tests with:
npm test

Note: Manages project dependencies and commands. 15. package-lock.json — Dependency lock file
Purpose: Records the exact dependency versions and dependency tree installed by npm.
This helps developers install consistent dependency versions across different machines and environments.
Note: Keeps dependency installation reproducible. Commit this file to Git. 16. Dockerfile — Container configuration
Purpose: Defines how to package your application into a Docker image.
Your Dockerfile:

- Uses Node.js 24 Alpine as the base image.
- Sets /app as the working directory.
- Installs production dependencies.
- Copies the source files into the image.
- Runs the application as a non-root user.
- Configures the CLI as the container's entry point.
  Example:
  docker build -t secure-password-tool .
  docker run --rm secure-password-tool generate --length 20

Note: Allows the application to run in a consistent, isolated environment without requiring the user to configure Node.js directly on the host machine. 17. README.md — Project documentation
Purpose: Explains the project to someone who wants to install, use, test, or review it.
It documents:

- Project features.
- Installation requirements.
- Environment configuration.
- CLI commands.
- Running tests.
- Docker usage.
- Security considerations.
- Project structure.
  Note: Helps another developer understand and run the application without asking you for instructions.

6. Quick revision table for your notes
   File One-line purpose
   validator.js Checks password strength rules.
   hibp.js Checks password hashes against HIBP breach data.
   generator.js Creates cryptographically random passwords.
   safe-generator.js Generates passwords and retries if they appear in breach data.
   slack.js Sends generated passwords to Slack.
   cli.js Connects modules and handles terminal commands.
   validator.test.js Tests password validation.
   hibp.test.js Tests breach-checking behavior.
   generator.test.js Tests password generation.
   slack.test.js Tests Slack delivery behavior.
   safe-generator.test.js Tests retries and fail-safe behavior.
   .env Stores local configuration and secrets.
   .gitignore Excludes files from Git.
   package.json Defines dependencies and scripts.
   package-lock.json Locks dependency versions.
   Dockerfile Defines the Docker image.
   README.md Documents setup and usage.
   The main concept to remember: cli.js coordinates the application, each file in src/ has a specific responsibility, and the test files verify that those responsibilities work as expected.
